WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.640
A crowd of small follicles starts to grow.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.680
A follicle is an oocyte with the cells around it.

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.060
Yet one oocyte is released, at a predictable time after a hormone surge.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.880
How does the ovary turn a crowd into one?

5
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.600
First, FSH picks one follicle.

6
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.080
Then that follicle's estradiol turns a brake into an accelerator.

7
00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:26.333
At the end of the previous cycle, FSH rises.

8
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:32.313
It rescues a cohort of small antral follicles from atresia, the degeneration that

9
00:00:32.313 --> 00:00:33.913
ends almost every follicle.

10
00:00:34.393 --> 00:00:39.593
The rescued follicles make inhibin B, which suppresses pituitary FSH.

11
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:43.313
The cohort lowers the very hormone that saved it.

12
00:00:43.653 --> 00:00:49.093
As FSH falls, only the follicle most sensitive to FSH keeps growing.

13
00:00:49.093 --> 00:00:50.613
It becomes dominant.

14
00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:52.493
The rest undergo atresia.

15
00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:57.363
In the human follicle before ovulation, two cell types share the work.

16
00:00:58.003 --> 00:01:04.743
Theca cells, outside the basement membrane, carry LH receptors and an enzyme, CYP17.

17
00:01:05.503 --> 00:01:06.503
They make androgen.

18
00:01:07.143 --> 00:01:12.763
Granulosa cells, inside, carry FSH receptors and aromatase, which converts the

19
00:01:12.763 --> 00:01:13.863
androgen to estrogen.

20
00:01:14.523 --> 00:01:19.383
As granulosa cells mature under FSH, they gain LH receptors.

21
00:01:20.163 --> 00:01:25.763
In the model, the most mature follicle can respond to LH while FSH falls.

22
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:29.207
For most of the cycle, estradiol is a brake.

23
00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:32.867
It lowers GnRH release and LH release.

24
00:01:33.447 --> 00:01:35.847
Late in the follicular phase, the sign reverses.

25
00:01:36.227 --> 00:01:40.347
When estradiol from the dominant follicle stays high for long enough, it becomes

26
00:01:40.347 --> 00:01:44.387
positive feedback, and LH climbs steeply in the LH surge.

27
00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:48.707
Most mechanistic work is in mice, so human circuitry is less certain.

28
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.710
In this sketch, FSH rises at the end of the previous cycle and falls through the mid

29
00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:55.890
-follicular phase.

30
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.130
Estradiol climbs to a late-follicular peak.

31
00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.190
LH surges within a day of that peak.

32
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:09.050
Textbooks place surge onset roughly 34 to 36 hours before ovulation, and intervals

33
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:14.990
vary widely. Progesterone begins to rise shortly before the surge and climbs after

34
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:18.670
ovulation, with a second, smaller rise of estradiol.

35
00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.490
At the cycle's end, FSH rises again.

36
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:26.733
After ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone.

37
00:02:27.293 --> 00:02:32.673
In a 1984 study, blood was drawn every 10 minutes for 24 hours from normally cycling

38
00:02:32.673 --> 00:02:38.653
women. In the mid and late luteal phase, progesterone rose in episodes after LH

39
00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:45.713
pulses. Across the sampled women in times, values ranged from 2.3 to 40.1

40
00:02:45.713 --> 00:02:47.113
nanograms per millilitre.

41
00:02:47.793 --> 00:02:50.753
One sample cannot measure a day of luteal output.

42
00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:55.473
Read a pulsatile hormone as a sequence in time, not from one sample.
